= Programming
[[147]edit]
[148]
[149] Image: Block Diagram
ESP32 function block diagram
Programming languages, frameworks, platforms, and
environments used for ESP32 programming:
* ESP-IDF[53][54] – Espressif's official IoT Development
Framework for the ESP32, ESP32-S, ESP32-C and ESP32-H
series of SoCs.
* [150]Arduino-ESP32[55] – Arduino core for the ESP32,
ESP32-S2, ESP32-S3 and ESP32-C3.
* ESP32forth[56] – [151]FORTH implementation for ESP32
* [152]Espruino – JavaScript SDK and firmware closely
emulating [153]Node.js
* [154]MicroPython (and [155]CircuitPython) – lean
implementation of Python 3 for microcontrollers
* [156]Mongoose OS – an operating system for connected
products on microcontrollers; programmable with JavaScript
or C. A recommended platform by Espressif Systems,[57] AWS
IoT,[58] and Google Cloud IoT.[59]
* [157]mruby and [158]picoruby for the ESP32
* [159]Nim for the ESP32
* [160]NodeMCU – [161]Lua-based firmware
* [162]Rust[60][61]
* [163]Swift[62][63]
* [164]Visual Studio Code with the officially supported
Espressif Integrated Development Framework (ESP-IDF)
Extension[64]
* [165]Zerynth – Python for IoT and microcontrollers,
including the ESP32
* Matlab [166]Simulink
* [167]Zig for the ESP32[65]
= Reception and use
[[168]edit]
Commercial, industrial and academic uses of ESP32:
= Use in commercial devices
[[169]edit]
* [170]Alibaba Group's IoT LED wristband, used by
participants at the group's 2017 annual gathering. Each
wristband operated as a "pixel", receiving commands for
coordinated LED light control, allowing formation of a
"live and wireless" screen.[66]
* DingTalk's M1, a biometric attendance-tracking
system.[67]
* Pium, a home fragrance and aromatherapy device.[68]
* HardKernel's [171]Odroid Go, an ESP32 based handheld
gaming device kit made to commemorate Odroid's 10th
anniversary.[69]
* [172]Playdate, a handheld video game console jointly
developed by [173]Panic Inc. and [174]Teenage Engineering.
* [175]Octopus Energy Mini, an ESP32-C6 based real-time
energy monitor.[70]
* Mysa smart thermostats, based on ESP32-WROOM.[71]
* Low-end UniFi switches from [176]Ubiquiti, based on
ESP32-D0WD-V3.[72]
* [177]Xteink X4, a compact e-ink reader with support for
community firmware.[73][74]
= Use in industrial devices
[[178]edit]
* TECHBASE's Moduino X series X1 and X2 modules are
ESP32-WROVER / ESP32-WROVER-B based computers for
industrial automation and monitoring, supporting digital
inputs/outputs, analog inputs, and various computer
networking interfaces.[75]
* NORVI IIOT Industrial Devices with ESP32-WROVER /
ESP32-WROVER-B SOC for industrial automation and
monitoring with digital inputs, analog inputs, relay
outputs and multiple communications interfaces. Supports
LoRa and Nb-IoT as expansion modules.[76]
= Academic uses
[[179]edit]
* ESP32 devices are utilized in educational settings[77]
and academic research projects. Additionally, ESP32 is
used in DIY projects such as building low-cost drones.[78]
The DJI Robomaster TT, an educational variant of the
[180]Ryze Tello drone, is equipped with an ESP32.[79]
= Undocumented HCI commands in ESP32
[[181]edit]
In March 2025, researchers from Tarlogic Security
identified undocumented Host Controller Interface (HCI)
commands in the ESP32 Bluetooth firmware, prompting
discussions about their functionality and potential
implications.[80] This discovery was presented on March 6,
2025, at the [182]RootedCON conference by the Tarlogic
Security team.[81]
The identified commands, such as _Write Memory_ (0xFC02),
are vendor-specific HCI commands used primarily for
debugging and testing purposes. These types of commands
are common in Bluetooth controller implementations to
assist with development and troubleshooting. They are not
part of the standard HCI command set and are typically
used in controlled environments.[82] While initially
described as a "backdoor," further clarifications labeled
them as "undocumented debugging features." These commands
are not accessible remotely via standard Bluetooth
connections but could be interacted with if an entity has
physical access to the device or operates in an HCI-UART
configuration.
Espressif Systems provided clarification regarding these
commands, stating that they are intended for debugging and
do not pose a security risk under normal operating
conditions. The company emphasized that these commands
cannot be triggered remotely and are not used in standard
Bluetooth operations. These commands are present only in
ESP32 chips and are not included in the ESP32-C, ESP32-S,
and ESP32-H series. To address concerns raised within the
security community, Espressif announced that future
versions of the ESP-IDF would include updates to restrict
access to these debugging commands and improve
documentation for vendor-specific HCI commands. These
actions aim to provide additional transparency and ensure
developers are well-informed about available
functionalities.[83]
